Review whether the implementation below is sufficient for its stated purpose. Report actionable findings with their impact and smallest correction; do not rewrite it. If there are no actionable findings, say so. Assess actual requirements, not an imagined future product.

Purpose: a developer manually sends one trusted local task to a synchronous worker callable and receives the returned text. One call at a time. The host handles transport timeouts. The task and result have no required durable state; the operator copies useful output into the existing project. There are no live, destructive, security-sensitive or financial effects. The operator sees failures and decides whether to retry. worker has the contract worker(task: str) -> str and can raise an ordinary exception. The caller consumes the returned status and displays result or error plus next_action verbatim. No unattended recovery, concurrent writers or alternative consumers are required.

```python
def run_job(task, worker):
    if not isinstance(task, str) or not task.strip():
        raise ValueError("Supply a nonempty task string.")
    try:
        result = worker(task)
    except Exception as error:
        return {"status": "failed", "error": str(error),
                "next_action": "Inspect the worker error and retry manually when corrected."}
    if not isinstance(result, str) or not result.strip():
        return {"status": "failed", "error": "Worker returned no usable text.",
                "next_action": "Correct the worker to return a nonempty string, then retry."}
    return {"status": "complete", "result": result}
```

Existing direct checks prove success returns the worker text, invalid tasks never call the worker, exceptions are reported as failed, and blank/non-text results are rejected. No additional external context or files exist.
